  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nOver the next few days, an upper level ridge will build from the \nWest coast and remain over parts of Montana, allowing for \ntemperatures to increase into the low to mid 90s Friday and \nSaturday. With the increasing temperatures, the minimum relative \nhumidity will drop to the teens in the afternoons. The region will\nremain dry through the week and into the weekend. \n\nOn Friday, winds will increase aloft as the ridge begins to \nflatten. Daytime mixing will bring the higher winds down to the \nsurface, primarily across portions of Southwest and Central \nMontana, resulting in some gusty conditions in the area through \nSaturday and as humidity dips into the teens. These hot, breezy, \nand dry conditions will result in elevated to near critical fire \nweather conditions. \n\nOn Monday, a westerly flow aloft will cause temperatures to cool \ndown closer to seasonal averages, but will remain dry with low \nchances of precipitation. -Kiffe/Ludwig\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFire Weather Friday/Saturday: Low RH values and pockets of breezy\nwinds are still expected Friday and Saturday, though the threat \nappears to have reduced over the past few days. While the upper \nlevel ridge still looks to flatten out a bit on Friday, high \npressure at the surface looks to build over the plains, which will\nprevent some of the winds from reaching the surface across the \nplains, but some gusty winds remain possible along the Rocky \nMountain Front and into Southwestern Montana. However, at this \npoint wind gusts over 20 mph look neither frequent nor widespread \nenough to warrant a Red Flag Warning or Fire Weather Watch at this\ntime, but this will continue to be evaluated over the coming \ndays. Ludwig",
